<fe-compose name="PlatformPage">
  <fe-compose name="SubNav"></fe-compose>
  <section id="content-poc">
    <h2><span style="color:var(--card-b)">۞</span> Proof of concept</h2>
    <p>
      <em>For the following examples, assume you work for the **Conqueso** company/org.</em>
    </p>
    <p>
      The fastest path to a running platform: two MFEs, one shell, everything local.
      No CDN, no CI/CD, no cloud setup required. This walkthrough takes you from a
      fresh workspace to a working browser demo.
    </p>
    <h3>1 · Install and scaffold</h3>
    <textarea lang="shell">
bun add -d @fe/cli
fe new conqueso/mfe-a
fe new conqueso/mfe-b
</textarea>
    <p>
      Each <code>fe new</code> creates a directory with <code>package.json</code>,
      <code>tsconfig.json</code>, and a <code>src/index.ts</code> with a
      <code>render()</code> stub. Give <code>mfe-b</code> a dependency on
      <code>mfe-a</code>:
    </p>
    <textarea lang="shell">
fe link mfe-b mfe-a
</textarea>
    <h3>2 · Write a minimal shell</h3>
    <p>
      Create <code>host-app/index.html</code> with the platform config embedded and
      <code>host-app/src/index.ts</code> that loads the runtime:
    </p>
    <textarea lang="html">
<script type="application/json" id="platform-config">
  { "routes": {}, "packages": {} }
</script>
<script type="module" src="./src/index.ts"></script>
</textarea>
    <textarea lang="ts">
// host-app/src/index.ts
import { load, loadDevtools } from "@fe/runtime";
await loadDevtools();
await load(location.pathname);
</textarea>
    <h3>3 · Configure</h3>
    <p>
      Create <code>configs/fe.config.json</code> at the workspace root:
    </p>
    <textarea lang="json">
{
  "shellDir": "host-app",
  "manifestPath": "configs/platform.json",
  "jitPlugins": ["@fe/jit-plugin-react"]
}
</textarea>
    <p>
      Create <code>configs/platform.json</code> with empty routes and packages:
    </p>
    <textarea lang="json">
{
  "routes": {},
  "packages": {}
}
</textarea>
    <h3>4 · Publish both MFEs</h3>
    <textarea lang="shell">
fe publish mfe-a
fe publish mfe-b
</textarea>
    <p>
      Each command uploads source to <code>sources/</code> and adds an entry to
      <code>platform.json</code>. You will see version numbers printed to stdout.
    </p>
    <h3>5 · Add a route and serve</h3>
    <p>
      Edit <code>configs/platform.json</code> to map a route:
    </p>
    <textarea lang="json">
{
  "routes": { "/": "@conqueso/fe-mfe-b@1.0.0" },
  "packages": { ... }
}
</textarea>
    <textarea lang="shell">
fe build shell
fe serve
</textarea>
    <p>
      Open <code>http://localhost:3000</code>. The runtime resolves the route, injects
      import maps for both <code>mfe-b</code> and its dep <code>mfe-a</code>, and
      calls <code>render()</code>. The JIT server compiles each MFE on first request
      and caches the result.
    </p>
    <h3>What CI/CD automates from here</h3>
    <p>
      The manual steps above map directly to CI jobs: run <code>fe check</code> on
      every push, run <code>fe publish</code> on merge to main, and update the route
      in <code>platform.json</code> via a CD step. Each team does this independently
      for their own MFE.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="content-cloud">
    <h2><span style="color:var(--card-b)">۞</span> Cloud deployment</h2>
    <h3>CDN and caching</h3>
    <p>
      JIT-compiled artifacts are served with <code>Cache-Control: immutable</code>
      because the URL includes the version. Put a CDN in front of <code>fe serve</code>
      with the following origin configuration:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Forward all <code>/bundle/</code> requests to the JIT server origin.</li>
      <li>Cache responses that include <code>Cache-Control: immutable</code> at the edge.</li>
      <li>The first request per version compiles; every subsequent request serves the CDN copy.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Content Security Policy</h3>
    <p>
      The minimum required CSP for a fe-based shell:
    </p>
    <textarea lang="shell">
# Content-Security-Policy:
  script-src 'self' https://cdn.your-domain.com;
  script-src-elem 'self' https://cdn.your-domain.com;
</textarea>
    <p>
      Import maps require no special directives beyond <code>script-src</code>. If
      any MFE uses WebAssembly, add <code>'wasm-unsafe-eval'</code> to
      <code>script-src</code>.
    </p>
    <h3>Config management</h3>
    <p>
      Two config files serve different audiences:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>File</th>
          <th>Who edits it</th>
          <th>What it controls</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>fe.config.json</code></td>
          <td>Platform team</td>
          <td>JIT plugins, storage adapters, shell directory</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>platform.json</code></td>
          <td>CD pipeline (or platform team)</td>
          <td>Routes and package versions</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The platform team controls the JIT server configuration. MFE teams never edit
      <code>fe.config.json</code>. The CD pipeline updates <code>platform.json</code>
      when a new version is published.
    </p>
    <h3>Rollback</h3>
    <p>
      Because each publish creates a new immutable URL, rollback is a one-line change
      to <code>platform.json</code>. Old versions remain available forever. There is no
      "deploy" step that overwrites a previous version.
    </p>
    <textarea lang="json">
// platform.json (before)
"routes": { "/": "@conqueso/fe-mfe-a@1.2.0" }
// platform.json (after rollback)
"routes": { "/": "@conqueso/fe-mfe-a@1.1.0" }
</textarea>
    <p>
      The runtime reads the updated manifest on the next page load. No server restart
      required.
    </p>
    <h3>Monitoring and observability</h3>
    <p>
      The JIT server logs each compilation with timing and cache status. Integrate
      these logs with your observability stack:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cache hit rate</strong> — should approach 100% after warmup.</li>
      <li><strong>Compilation time</strong> — typically 50-200ms per MFE.</li>
      <li><strong>Error rate</strong> — should be near zero; investigate any spike.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section id="content-enterprise">
    <h2><span style="color:var(--card-b)">۞</span> Enterprise deployment</h2>
    <h3>Private registry</h3>
    <p>
      MFEs can be published to a private registry instead of the local
      <code>sources/</code> directory. Implement a <code>SourceStorage</code> adapter
      that reads from your artifact storage:
    </p>
    <textarea lang="ts">
// @conqueso/fe-plugin-s3/src/index.ts
import type { Plugin, CliContext } from "@fe/core";
import { S3Client } from "@aws-sdk/client-s3";
class S3SourceStorage implements SourceStorage {
  private client = new S3Client({ region: "us-east-1" });
  async read(slug: string, version: string): Promise<string> {
    const key = `${slug}/${version}/index.ts`;
    const resp = await this.client.getObject({ Bucket: "conqueso-mfe-sources", Key: key });
    return await resp.Body.transformToString();
  }
}
export default {
  name: "conqueso-s3",
  setup(ctx: CliContext) {
    ctx.adapters.sourceStorage = new S3SourceStorage();
  }
} satisfies Plugin;
</textarea>
    <p>
      Declare the plugin in <code>fe.config.json</code>:
    </p>
    <textarea lang="json">
{
  "plugins": ["@conqueso/fe-plugin-s3"]
}
</textarea>
    <h3>Authentication</h3>
    <p>
      The platform does not prescribe an auth mechanism. MFEs can implement their own
      auth flows, or the shell can inject an auth context into each <code>render()</code>
      call via the <code>props</code> argument.
    </p>
    <textarea lang="ts">
// host-app/src/index.ts
import { load } from "@fe/runtime";
import { getAuthState } from "./auth";
await load(location.pathname, {
  auth: getAuthState()
});
</textarea>
    <p>
      Each MFE receives the auth state in its <code>render()</code> props.
    </p>
    <h3>Multi-tenant hosting</h3>
    <p>
      To host multiple tenants on a single platform instance, use route prefixes:
    </p>
    <textarea lang="json">
{
  "routes": {
    "/tenant-a/": "@conqueso/fe-tenant-a-shell@1.0.0",
    "/tenant-b/": "@conqueso/fe-tenant-b-shell@1.0.0"
  }
}
</textarea>
    <p>
      Each tenant shell can load a completely different set of MFEs. The runtime
      resolves routes independently for each path prefix.
    </p>
    <h3>Plugin development</h3>
    <p>
      Plugins can swap any adapter at bootstrap time. Common use cases:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Artifact storage</strong> — replace local <code>uploads/</code> with S3, GCS, or Azure Blob.</li>
      <li><strong>Config provider</strong> — read <code>fe.config.json</code> from environment variables or a remote API.</li>
      <li><strong>CLI commands</strong> — add new commands like <code>fe deploy</code> or <code>fe status</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Plugins run after built-in adapters are initialized, so they can freely
      override any adapter.
    </p>
  </section>
</fe-compose>
</file>

